(function () {
  const imported = [{"id":"numbers","title":"Number Systems & the Real Line","src":["CamScanner 16-09-2026 (scan)"],"notes":"\n<h3>The chain of number sets</h3>\n<p>Each set sits inside the next: <b>ℕ ⊂ ℤ ⊂ ℚ ⊂ ℝ ⊂ ℂ</b>.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Natural numbers ℕ</b>: counting numbers 1, 2, 3, …</li>\n<li><b>Integers ℤ</b>: …, −2, −1, 0, 1, 2, …</li>\n<li><b>Rational numbers ℚ</b>: numbers that can be written p/q (q ≠ 0). Their decimals terminate or repeat.</li>\n<li><b>Irrational numbers</b>: cannot be written as p/q (e.g. √2, π). Their decimals never end and never repeat.</li>\n<li><b>Real numbers ℝ</b>: rationals together with irrationals.</li>\n<li><b>Complex numbers ℂ</b>: a + bi where i² = −1. Every real number is complex (b = 0).</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>The real number line</h3>\n<p>Every real number matches exactly one point on a line. Numbers increase to the right, so −4 &lt; −3 &lt; … &lt; 0 &lt; 1 &lt; 2.</p>\n<div class=\"box\">Quick test: √(perfect square) is rational (√16 = 4). √(non-perfect square) is irrational (√2, √3, √5…).</div>","cards":[["What is the chain of number sets?","ℕ ⊂ ℤ ⊂ ℚ ⊂ ℝ ⊂ ℂ"],["Define a rational number.","A number that can be written p/q with integers p, q and q ≠ 0. Decimal terminates or repeats."],["Define an irrational number.","A real number that cannot be written as p/q. Decimal never ends and never repeats (e.g. √2, π)."],["What do rationals and irrationals make together?","The set of real numbers ℝ."],["Is every real number also a complex number?","Yes. Write it as a + 0i."],["What is i?","The imaginary unit, i = √−1, so i² = −1."],["Which set contains −3 but not 1/2?","The integers ℤ (1/2 is rational but not an integer)."],["Is 0.333… rational?","Yes. It repeats, and equals 1/3."],["Is √16 rational or irrational?","Rational, because √16 = 4."],["On the real number line, which is further left: −4 or −1?","−4. Numbers increase to the right."]],"qs":[["Which of these is irrational?",["0.25","√2","22/7","√9"],1,"√2 is not a perfect-square root, so it cannot be written as p/q. 0.25 = 1/4, 22/7 is a fraction, √9 = 3."],["The smallest of these sets that contains −3 is",["ℕ","ℤ","ℚ","ℝ"],1,"−3 is a negative integer, and it is not in ℕ. ℤ is the smallest set listed that contains it."],["0.333… is",["Irrational","Rational","Imaginary","Not a number"],1,"A repeating decimal equals a fraction: 0.333… = 1/3."],["Rationals together with irrationals form",["ℕ","ℤ","ℝ","ℂ"],2,"By definition, ℚ ∪ (irrationals) = ℝ."],["Which is NOT a real number?",["−7","√3","i","π"],2,"i = √−1 is imaginary. It lies in ℂ, not ℝ."],["Which statement is true?",["ℝ ⊂ ℚ","ℤ ⊂ ℕ","ℚ ⊂ ℝ","ℂ ⊂ ℝ"],2,"Every rational is real, so ℚ ⊂ ℝ. The others are reversed."],["√49 is",["Irrational","Rational","Imaginary","Undefined"],1,"√49 = 7, which is an integer, hence rational."],["On the number line, which number is furthest to the right?",["−1","−3","0","−4"],2,"Numbers increase to the right. 0 is greater than all the negatives."],["7/2 belongs to",["ℤ only","ℚ but not ℤ","ℕ","Imaginary numbers"],1,"7/2 = 3.5 is a fraction, so rational, but it is not an integer."],["Every real number is also a",["Natural number","Integer","Complex number","Irrational number"],2,"A real number x equals x + 0i, which is complex."]]},{"id":"sets","title":"Set Theory","src":["mat101_sets.pdf","Maths MCQ.pdf (Sets)","Maths Exercise.pdf","Practice Questions - Sets.docx (topic only)"],"notes":"\n<h3>Basics</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>A <b>set</b> is a collection of objects (elements). x ∈ A means \"x belongs to A\".</li>\n<li><b>List form</b>: A = {1, 2, 3}. <b>Predicate form</b>: B = {x : x is a negative integer greater than −4}.</li>\n<li><b>Empty set</b>: ∅ or { }. It is a subset of every set.</li>\n<li><b>Subset</b> B ⊆ A: every element of B is in A. Every set is a subset of itself (improper subset); others are <b>proper</b>.</li>\n<li>If A ⊆ B and B ⊆ A then A = B.</li>\n<li>A set with n elements has <b>2<sup>n</sup></b> subsets and <b>2<sup>n</sup> − 1</b> proper subsets. The power set P(A) has 2<sup>n</sup> elements.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Operations</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Intersection</b> A ∩ B: elements in both.</li>\n<li><b>Union</b> A ∪ B: elements in A or B (or both).</li>\n<li><b>Disjoint</b>: A ∩ B = ∅.</li>\n<li><b>Universal set</b> U: the background set. <b>Complement</b> A′ = {x ∈ U : x ∉ A}.</li>\n<li><b>Relative complement</b> A − B = {a ∈ A : a ∉ B}.</li>\n<li><b>Symmetric difference</b> A Δ B = (A − B) ∪ (B − A). In A or B but not both.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Boolean algebra laws</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>Commutative: A ∩ B = B ∩ A, A ∪ B = B ∪ A</li>\n<li>Idempotent: A ∩ A = A, A ∪ A = A</li>\n<li>Associative: A ∩ (B ∩ C) = (A ∩ B) ∩ C</li>\n<li>Distributive: A ∩ (B ∪ C) = (A ∩ B) ∪ (A ∩ C), A ∪ (B ∩ C) = (A ∪ B) ∩ (A ∪ C)</li>\n<li><b>De Morgan</b>: (A ∩ B)′ = A′ ∪ B′ and (A ∪ B)′ = A′ ∩ B′</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Counting with Venn diagrams</h3>\n<div class=\"box\">n(A ∪ B) = n(A) + n(B) − n(A ∩ B)<br>n(A ∪ B ∪ C) = n(A) + n(B) + n(C) − n(A∩B) − n(B∩C) − n(A∩C) + n(A∩B∩C)</div>\n<p><b>Method:</b> fill the centre (all three) first, then the pair overlaps, then the \"only\" regions, and finally the outside region.</p>\n<h3>Worked examples from the handout</h3>\n<p><b>Physics &amp; Economics.</b> 90 students, 45 physics, 58 economics, 10 take neither. Then 80 take at least one. 45 + 58 − x = 80, so x = 23 take both. Economics only = 58 − 23 = 35.</p>\n<p><b>Three subjects.</b> 200 students; 70 physics, 90 chemistry, 100 maths; 24 none; 23 P&amp;C; 41 C&amp;M; 8 all three. Union = 176, so 176 = 70 + 90 + 100 − 23 − 41 − (P&amp;M) + 8, giving P&amp;M = 28. Exactly two = (23−8) + (41−8) + (28−8) = 15 + 33 + 20 = <b>68</b>.</p>\n<p><b>Soft drinks (100 people).</b> Solving gives a=18, e=5, g=3, f=5, c=35, d=0, b=1, none=33. Likers of B = b + d + f + g = 1 + 0 + 5 + 3 = <b>9</b>. (The handout prints 35 for this total, which does not match its own working.)</p>","cards":[["Number of subsets of a set with n elements?","2ⁿ"],["Number of proper subsets of a set with n elements?","2ⁿ − 1"],["Size of the power set of a 5-element set?","2⁵ = 32"],["Define A ∩ B.","The set of elements that belong to both A and B."],["Define A ∪ B.","The set of elements in A or in B (or both)."],["When are A and B disjoint?","When A ∩ B = ∅."],["Define the complement A′.","All elements of the universal set U that are not in A."],["Define the relative complement A − B.","Elements of A that are not in B."],["Define the symmetric difference A Δ B.","(A − B) ∪ (B − A): in A or B but not both."],["State De Morgan's laws.","(A ∩ B)′ = A′ ∪ B′ and (A ∪ B)′ = A′ ∩ B′."],["Formula for n(A ∪ B)?","n(A) + n(B) − n(A ∩ B)"],["Formula for n(A ∪ B ∪ C)?","n(A)+n(B)+n(C) − n(A∩B) − n(B∩C) − n(A∩C) + n(A∩B∩C)"],["Which set is a subset of every set?","The empty set ∅."],["If A ⊆ B and B ⊆ A, what follows?","A = B."],["Distributive law for ∩ over ∪?","A ∩ (B ∪ C) = (A ∩ B) ∪ (A ∩ C)"],["Best first step in a three-set Venn problem?","Fill the centre region (A ∩ B ∩ C) first, then work outwards."]],"qs":[["If a set A has n elements, the number of subsets is",["n","2n","n²","2ⁿ"],3,"Each element is either in or out of a subset: 2 × 2 × … × 2 = 2ⁿ."],["The number of proper subsets of a 4-element set is",["8","15","16","4"],1,"2⁴ − 1 = 15 (remove the set itself)."],["The power set of a set with 3 elements has how many elements?",["3","6","8","9"],2,"2³ = 8."],["A ∪ ∅ =",["∅","U","A","A′"],2,"Adding nothing to A leaves A."],["The complement of the universal set U is",["U","∅","A","A′"],1,"U′ contains nothing outside U, so it is empty."],["If A ∩ B = ∅, then A and B are",["Equal","Universal","Disjoint","Finite"],2,"No common elements means disjoint."],["If A ⊆ B, then",["A ∪ B = A","A ∩ B = A","A = B","B ⊂ A"],1,"All of A lies inside B, so their overlap is A."],["If A ∪ B = A, then",["A ⊂ B","B ⊆ A","A = B","A ∩ B = ∅"],1,"Union adds nothing, so everything in B was already in A."],["Which law states A ∪ B = B ∪ A?",["Associative","Distributive","Commutative","Identity"],2,"Order does not matter: commutative law."],["A ∩ A =",["∅","U","A","A′"],2,"Idempotent law."],["Let A = {1, 2}, B = {2, 3}. Then A ∩ B =",["{1}","{2}","{3}","∅"],1,"Only 2 appears in both."],["Let A = {1,2,3,4,5,6}, B = {4,5,6,7,8}. A Δ B =",["{4,5,6}","{1,2,3,7,8}","{1,2,3,4,5,6,7,8}","{7,8}"],1,"Remove the common part {4,5,6} from the union: {1,2,3,7,8}."],["Let A = {x ∈ ℕ : 2 < x < 9} and B = {x ∈ ℕ : 5 ≤ x < 14}. A ∩ B =",["{3,4}","{5,6,7,8}","{5,…,13}","{3,…,13}"],1,"A = {3,…,8}, B = {5,…,13}. Overlap: {5,6,7,8}."],["(A ∪ B)′ equals",["A′ ∪ B′","A′ ∩ B′","A ∩ B","A − B"],1,"De Morgan's law."],["Which is NOT a well-defined set?",["Even numbers","Prime numbers","Tall students","Integers"],2,"“Tall” has no precise rule, so membership is unclear."],["U = {1,…,10}, A = {2,4,6,8,10}, B = {2,3,5,7}. A′ ∩ B′ =",["{1,9}","{1,3,5,7,9}","{3,5,7}","{2}"],0,"A ∪ B = {2,3,4,5,6,7,8,10}. Its complement in U is {1, 9}."],["In a class of 90, 45 take physics, 58 economics and 10 take neither. How many take both?",["13","23","33","35"],1,"At least one: 80. 45 + 58 − x = 80, so x = 23."],["In the same class (90, 45 P, 58 E, 10 neither), how many take economics only?",["23","35","45","58"],1,"Economics only = 58 − 23 = 35."],["200 students: 70 physics, 90 chem, 100 maths, 24 none, 23 P&C, 41 C&M, 8 all three. How many take exactly two subjects?",["60","68","76","92"],1,"Union = 176. P&M = 70+90+100+8−176−23−41 = 28. Exactly two = 15 + 33 + 20 = 68."],["39 friends each own a dog, cat or rabbit: 24 dogs, 17 cats, 16 rabbits; dog&cat is 1 more than cat&rabbit; 9 dog&rabbit; 2 all three. How many have cat and rabbit?",["4","5","6","7"],1,"39 = 57 − (c + 1 + c + 9) + 2 where c = cat&rabbit. So 2c = 10 and c = 5."],["Of 140 freshmen, 46 Accounting, 56 Statistics, 44 CS, 12 A&S, 14 S&CS, 16 A&CS, 25 none. How many registered for at least one?",["95","105","115","125"],2,"140 − 25 = 115."],["For that survey, how many are in all three departments?",["9","10","11","12"],2,"115 = 146 − 42 + x, so x = 11."],["For that survey, how many registered for just one department?",["85","90","95","100"],2,"A only = 46−12−16+11 = 29; S only = 56−12−14+11 = 41; CS only = 44−16−14+11 = 25. Total 95."],["For that survey, how many registered for Statistics and CS but not Accounting?",["3","5","11","14"],0,"14 − 11 = 3."],["U = {−10,…,10}, A = multiples of 3, B = primes, C = roots of x⁴+2x³−5x²−6x = 0. Find A − (B ∪ C).",["{−9,−6,6,9}","{−9,−6,−3,6,9}","{−9,−6,0,6,9}","{−9,9}"],0,"C: x(x+1)(x−2)(x+3) = 0, so C = {0,−1,2,−3}. B = {2,3,5,7}. Remove 0, −3 and 3 from A."]]},{"id":"ap","title":"Arithmetic Progression (AP)","src":["Sequence and Series.pdf","MTH 101_Elementary Mathematics_I (2).pdf","Maths MCQ.pdf (AP & GP)","Maths Exercise.pdf"],"notes":"\n<h3>Key ideas</h3>\n<p>A <b>sequence</b> is an ordered list of numbers following a rule. The sum of its terms is a <b>series</b>. An <b>AP</b> has a constant <b>common difference d</b> between consecutive terms.</p>\n<div class=\"box\">\nnth term: T<sub>n</sub> = a + (n − 1)d<br>\nSum of n terms: S<sub>n</sub> = (n/2)[2a + (n − 1)d]<br>\nWith last term l: S<sub>n</sub> = (n/2)(a + l)\n</div>\n<ul>\n<li>d = T<sub>n</sub> − T<sub>n−1</sub>. If d &gt; 0 the AP increases; if d &lt; 0 it decreases; if d = 0 it is constant.</li>\n<li>Arithmetic mean of a and b: (a + b)/2.</li>\n<li>Three numbers a, b, c are in AP when 2b = a + c.</li>\n<li>Trick for three terms in AP: use (a − d), a, (a + d).</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Derivation of S<sub>n</sub></h3>\n<p>Write the sum forwards and backwards, then add. Each pair of terms adds to 2a + (n−1)d, and there are n pairs, so 2S<sub>n</sub> = n(2a + (n−1)d).</p>\n<h3>Worked examples</h3>\n<p><b>Ex 1.</b> 15th term of 5, 8, 11, … : a = 5, d = 3, T<sub>15</sub> = 5 + 14×3 = 47.</p>\n<p><b>Ex 2.</b> Sum of first 20 terms of 3, 7, 11, … : T<sub>20</sub> = 3 + 19×4 = 79, S<sub>20</sub> = 10 × 82 = 820.</p>\n<p><b>Ex 3.</b> Ade saves ₦100 then adds ₦50 each month. After 12 months: S<sub>12</sub> = 6 × (200 + 550) = ₦4,500.</p>\n<p><b>Ex 4.</b> Sequence 4, 1, −2, −5, … has T<sub>n</sub> = 7 − 3n. T<sub>n</sub> = −23 gives n = 10 so −23 is a term. T<sub>n</sub> = −70 gives n = 77/3, not an integer, so −70 is not a term.</p>\n<p><b>Ex 5.</b> T<sub>12</sub> = 13 and T<sub>25</sub> = 52. Subtract: 13d = 39, d = 3, a = −20. AP: −20, −17, −14, …</p>","cards":[["nth term of an AP?","Tₙ = a + (n − 1)d"],["Sum of the first n terms of an AP?","Sₙ = (n/2)[2a + (n − 1)d]"],["Sum of an AP using the last term l?","Sₙ = (n/2)(a + l)"],["What is the common difference d?","d = Tₙ − Tₙ₋₁ (any term minus the one before it)."],["Condition for a, b, c to be in AP?","2b = a + c (b is the arithmetic mean of a and c)."],["Arithmetic mean of a and b?","(a + b)/2"],["What does d = 0 give?","A constant sequence."],["If d is negative, the AP is…","Decreasing."],["Handy way to write three terms in AP?","a − d, a, a + d"],["How do you find a and d from two given terms?","Write both as a + (k−1)d, then solve the two equations simultaneously."],["What is a series?","The sum of the terms of a sequence."],["Trick used to derive Sₙ?","Write the sum forwards and backwards, then add the two lines: 2Sₙ = n(2a + (n−1)d)."],["In an AP, the middle term equals…","The average of the first and last terms."]],"qs":[["The general term of an AP is",["a + nr","a + (n − 1)d","arⁿ","a/d"],1,"Tₙ = a + (n − 1)d."],["The 30th term of the AP 2, 5, 8, 11, … is",["87","89","92","95"],1,"a = 2, d = 3. T₃₀ = 2 + 29×3 = 89."],["The sum of the first 30 terms of 3, 8, 13, 18, … is",["2265","2250","2310","2415"],0,"a = 3, d = 5. S₃₀ = 15(6 + 145) = 15 × 151 = 2265."],["A factory makes 300 units in week 1 and raises output by 25 each week. Total after 15 weeks?",["6000","6750","7125","7500"],2,"S₁₅ = (15/2)(600 + 14×25) = 7.5 × 950 = 7125."],["Which term of the AP 2, 5, 8, … is 44?",["14th","15th","16th","17th"],1,"2 + (n−1)3 = 44 gives n − 1 = 14, n = 15."],["The sum of three consecutive terms of an AP is 6 and their product is −90. The terms are",["−5, 2, 9","−3, 2, 7","−6, 2, 10","−4, 2, 8"],0,"Use a−d, a, a+d: 3a = 6, a = 2. 2(4 − d²) = −90 gives d² = 49, d = 7. Terms: −5, 2, 9."],["The sum of the first ten terms of an AP is 60 and of the first twenty-two terms is 220. The first term is",["2","3","4","5"],1,"2a + 9d = 12 and 2a + 21d = 20. Subtract: 12d = 8, d = 2/3. Then a = 3."],["The 5th term of an AP is 23 and the 12th is 37. The sum of the first eleven terms is",["220","253","275","286"],2,"7d = 14, d = 2, a = 15. S₁₁ = (11/2)(30 + 20) = 275."],["The 4th term of an AP is −20 and the 8th is −10. The 100th term is",["200","210","220","230"],2,"4d = 10, d = 2.5, a = −27.5. T₁₀₀ = −27.5 + 99×2.5 = 220."],["In an AP, the sum of the 1st and 5th terms is 18 and the 5th term is 6 more than the 3rd. The sum of the first ten terms is",["135","150","165","180"],2,"2d = 6, d = 3. 2a + 12 = 18, a = 3. S₁₀ = 5(6 + 27) = 165."],["The sum of the first thirteen terms of 3 + 19 + 35 + … is",["1170","1287","1300","1352"],1,"d = 16. S₁₃ = (13/2)(6 + 12×16) = 6.5 × 198 = 1287."],["The common difference of an AP is",["Tₙ − Tₙ₋₁","Tₙ / Tₙ₋₁","a·r","a + r"],0,"Constant difference between consecutive terms."],["If d = 0, the AP is",["Increasing","Decreasing","Constant","Infinite"],2,"Every term equals a."],["If d is negative, the AP is",["Increasing","Decreasing","Constant","Alternating"],1,"Each term is smaller than the one before."],["The arithmetic mean between a and b is",["ab","(a + b)/2","√(ab)","a − b"],1,"Average of the two."],["Three numbers a, b, c are in AP when",["2b = a + c","b² = ac","a + b + c = 0","ab = c"],0,"b is the average of a and c."],["For the sequence 4, 1, −2, −5, … the nth term is",["7 − 3n","4 − 3n","3n + 1","n − 7"],0,"4 + (n−1)(−3) = 7 − 3n."],["Is −70 a term of 4, 1, −2, −5, …?",["Yes, the 77th","Yes, the 25th","No","Yes, the 10th"],2,"7 − 3n = −70 gives n = 77/3, not an integer, so −70 is not a term."],["Ade saves ₦100 in month 1 and increases savings by ₦50 each month. Total after 12 months?",["₦4,000","₦4,500","₦5,000","₦6,000"],1,"S₁₂ = 6(200 + 550) = ₦4,500."],["The sum of the first n terms of an AP is",["n(a + d)","(n/2)[2a + (n−1)d]","a·rⁿ","a + (n−1)d"],1,"Standard AP sum formula."],["The sum of the series Σ(4x − 5) for x = 1 to 7 is",["63","70","77","84"],2,"Terms: −1, 3, 7, 11, 15, 19, 23. Sum = (7/2)(−1 + 23) = 77."],["An AP is called “linear” because its terms change by a constant",["Ratio","Square","Difference","Product"],2,"Equal steps of d."]]},{"id":"gp","title":"Geometric Progression (GP) & Infinite Series","src":["Sequence and Series.pdf","MTH 101_Elementary Mathematics_I (2).pdf","Maths MCQ.pdf (AP & GP)","Maths Exercise.pdf"],"notes":"\n<h3>Key ideas</h3>\n<p>A <b>GP</b> has a constant <b>common ratio r</b> between consecutive terms: a, ar, ar², ar³, …</p>\n<div class=\"box\">\nnth term: U<sub>n</sub> = ar<sup>n−1</sup><br>\nSum of n terms: S<sub>n</sub> = a(1 − r<sup>n</sup>)/(1 − r), r ≠ 1<br>\nSum to infinity (only if |r| &lt; 1): S<sub>∞</sub> = a/(1 − r)\n</div>\n<ul>\n<li>r = U<sub>n</sub>/U<sub>n−1</sub>.</li>\n<li>a, b, c are in GP when b² = ac. Geometric mean of a and b is √(ab).</li>\n<li>If r &gt; 1 (a &gt; 0) terms grow; if r &lt; 0 the signs alternate; if r = −1 terms alternate between a and −a; if r = 1 it is constant.</li>\n<li>If |r| ≥ 1 the infinite series has <b>no sum to infinity</b>.</li>\n<li>Three terms in GP: a/r, a, ar.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Derivation of S<sub>n</sub></h3>\n<p>S = a + ar + … + ar<sup>n−1</sup>. Multiply by r and subtract: S − rS = a − ar<sup>n</sup>. Factor: S(1 − r) = a(1 − r<sup>n</sup>).</p>\n<h3>Worked examples</h3>\n<p><b>Ex 1.</b> 8th term of 2, 4, 8, … is 2×2⁷ = 256.</p>\n<p><b>Ex 2.</b> S<sub>5</sub> of 1, 3, 9, … = (1 − 3⁵)/(1 − 3) = 121.</p>\n<p><b>Ex 3.</b> U<sub>3</sub> = 72 and U<sub>8</sub> = 8/27. Divide: r⁵ = (8/27)/72 = 1/243, so r = 1/3, and a = 72 × 9 = 648.</p>\n<p><b>Ex 4.</b> 4, 2, 1, ½, … : S<sub>∞</sub> = 4/(1 − ½) = 8. For 5, 10, 20, … r = 2, so no sum to infinity.</p>\n<p><b>Bouncing ball.</b> Dropped from 10 m, rebounds to 80%: total distance = 10 + 2×(8 + 6.4 + …) = 10 + 2×8/(1 − 0.8) = 10 + 80 = 90 m.</p>","cards":[["nth term of a GP?","Uₙ = a·rⁿ⁻¹"],["Sum of the first n terms of a GP?","Sₙ = a(1 − rⁿ)/(1 − r), r ≠ 1"],["Sum to infinity of a GP?","S∞ = a/(1 − r), valid only when |r| < 1."],["How do you find r?","r = Uₙ / Uₙ₋₁ (a term divided by the one before)."],["When does a GP have a sum to infinity?","Only when |r| < 1."],["Condition for a, b, c to be in GP?","b² = ac"],["Geometric mean of a and b?","√(ab)"],["What happens to a GP when r = −1?","Terms alternate between a and −a."],["What happens when r is negative?","Signs alternate."],["What happens when r = 1?","Constant sequence; GP becomes a, a, a, …"],["What happens when r = 0?","Every term after the first is 0."],["Handy way to write three terms in GP?","a/r, a, ar"],["How to find a and r from two given terms?","Divide the larger-index term by the smaller to get rᵏ, solve for r, then back-substitute for a."],["First step of the derivation of Sₙ?","Multiply the sum by r and subtract: S − rS = a − arⁿ."],["Why is GP growth called exponential?","Terms change by a constant ratio, not a constant difference."]],"qs":[["The nth term of a GP is",["a + (n − 1)d","arⁿ⁻¹","arⁿ","a/d"],1,"Uₙ = arⁿ⁻¹."],["The 12th term of the GP 3, 9, 27, 81, … is",["177147","531441","1594323","59049"],1,"3 × 3¹¹ = 3¹² = 531441."],["The sum of the first 12 terms of 2, 6, 18, 54, … is",["531440","531441","1062882","265720"],0,"S₁₂ = 2(3¹² − 1)/(3 − 1) = 3¹² − 1 = 531440."],["The sum to infinity of 16, 8, 4, 2, … is",["24","30","32","64"],2,"r = ½. S∞ = 16/(1 − ½) = 32."],["A ball is dropped from 10 m and rebounds to 80% of its previous height each time. Total distance travelled?",["50 m","80 m","90 m","100 m"],2,"10 + 2(8)/(1 − 0.8) = 10 + 80 = 90 m."],["The sum to infinity of 1 − ⅔ + 4/9 − … is",["3/5","3/2","1/3","5/3"],0,"a = 1, r = −⅔. S∞ = 1/(1 + ⅔) = 3/5."],["The sum to infinity of 16 + 12 + 9 + … is",["48","64","72","Does not exist"],1,"r = ¾. S∞ = 16/(1 − ¾) = 64."],["The sum to infinity of 5, 10, 20, … is",["−5","5","Does not exist","10"],2,"r = 2, so |r| > 1: the series diverges."],["The 3rd and 6th terms of a GP are 108 and −32. The sum of the first 7 terms is",["463/3","154","243","−32/3"],0,"r³ = −32/108 = −8/27, so r = −⅔. a = 108/r² = 243. S₇ = 243(1 − r⁷)/(1 − r) = 463/3."],["The first three terms of a GP are 2x − 1, x + 1, x − 1. A valid x with a sum to infinity is",["0","5","−5","1"],1,"(x+1)² = (2x−1)(x−1) gives x² − 5x = 0, so x = 0 or 5. x = 0 gives r = −1 (no sum). x = 5 gives 9, 6, 4 with r = ⅔ and S∞ = 27."],["Three consecutive GP terms have product 343 and sum 49/2. The numbers are",["7/2, 7, 14","1, 7, 49","7, 14, 28","7/4, 7, 28"],0,"Take 7/r, 7, 7r. 7(1/r + 1 + r) = 49/2 gives 2r² − 5r + 2 = 0, so r = 2 or ½. Terms: 7/2, 7, 14."],["The 6th term of the GP 10, 5, 2.5, … is",["0.625","0.3125","1.25","0.15625"],1,"10 × (½)⁵ = 10/32 = 0.3125."],["In a GP the common ratio is",["Tₙ − Tₙ₋₁","Tₙ / Tₙ₋₁","a + d","a − r"],1,"Ratio of successive terms."],["The sum to infinity exists if",["r > 1","r = 1","|r| < 1","r = 0 only"],2,"The terms must shrink in size."],["Sum to infinity formula is",["a/(1 − r)","a(1 − r)","arⁿ","a + r"],0,"S∞ = a/(1 − r)."],["If r = −1, the GP terms are",["Constant","Increasing","Alternating","Zero"],2,"a, −a, a, −a, …"],["If a > 0 and r > 1, the GP is",["Constant","Increasing","Decreasing","Alternating"],1,"Each term is larger than the last."],["Three numbers a, b, c are in GP when",["2b = a + c","b² = ac","a + b + c = 0","a = b = c"],1,"b/a = c/b gives b² = ac."],["The geometric mean of a and b is",["ab","(a + b)/2","√(ab)","a/b"],2,"Square root of the product."],["If r = 0, the GP is",["Constant","Increasing","Decreasing","Zero after the first term"],3,"a, 0, 0, 0, …"],["Which sequence is a GP?",["2, 4, 6, 8","1, 3, 9, 27","1, 4, 9, 16","5, 3, 1, −1"],1,"Constant ratio 3. The others have constant difference or no pattern of equal ratios."],["The 8th term of 2, 4, 8, 16, … is",["128","256","512","64"],1,"2 × 2⁷ = 256."],["The sum of the first 5 terms of 1, 3, 9, 27, … is",["81","121","242","364"],1,"(3⁵ − 1)/2 = 242/2 = 121."]]},{"id":"quad","title":"Quadratic Equations: Solving Methods","src":["Theory of quadratic equation.pdf","MTH 101_Elementary Mathematics_I (2).pdf","Maths MCQ.pdf (Quadratics)","Maths Exercise.pdf"],"notes":"\n<h3>Standard form</h3>\n<p>ax² + bx + c = 0 with a ≠ 0. The solutions are the <b>roots</b>. Four methods: <b>factorisation, completing the square, the quadratic formula, graphing</b>.</p>\n<h3>Completing the square (steps)</h3>\n<ol>\n<li>Move c to the right: ax² + bx = −c.</li>\n<li>Divide by a: x² + (b/a)x = −c/a.</li>\n<li>Add (b/2a)² to both sides.</li>\n<li>Write the left side as (x + b/2a)².</li>\n<li>Take square roots (±).</li>\n<li>Solve for x.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>Doing this generally produces the <b>quadratic formula</b>:</p>\n<div class=\"box\">x = [−b ± √(b² − 4ac)] / 2a</div>\n<p><b>Example.</b> 2x² + 8x − 6 = 0 → x² + 4x = 3 → (x + 2)² = 7 → x = −2 ± √7.</p>\n<h3>Graph facts</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>The graph is a <b>parabola</b>.</li>\n<li>Axis of symmetry: x = −b/2a. The vertex lies on it.</li>\n<li>If a = 0 the equation becomes linear, not quadratic.</li>\n<li>A quadratic has exactly two roots (counting repeated and complex ones).</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Reducing to a quadratic</h3>\n<p>(2x² − x)² − 9(2x² − x) + 18 = 0. Let u = 2x² − x: u² − 9u + 18 = 0, so u = 3 or 6. Then 2x² − x − 3 = 0 gives x = 3/2 or −1, and 2x² − x − 6 = 0 gives x = 2 or −3/2.</p>","cards":[["General form of a quadratic equation?","ax² + bx + c = 0, with a ≠ 0."],["Quadratic formula?","x = [−b ± √(b² − 4ac)] / 2a"],["Name the four ways to solve a quadratic.","Factorisation, completing the square, quadratic formula, graphical method."],["First step of completing the square?","Move the constant c to the right-hand side."],["What do you add in completing the square?","The square of half the coefficient of x: (b/2a)² after dividing by a."],["What shape is the graph of a quadratic?","A parabola."],["Axis of symmetry of y = ax² + bx + c?","x = −b/(2a)"],["What happens if a = 0?","The equation becomes linear."],["How many roots does a quadratic have?","Two (they may be equal or complex)."],["Solve x² − 5x + 6 = 0.","(x − 2)(x − 3) = 0, so x = 2 or 3."],["Solve 5x² + 13x + 6 = 0.","Δ = 49, x = (−13 ± 7)/10, so x = −3/5 or −2."],["What is the trick for (2x²−x)² − 9(2x²−x) + 18 = 0?","Substitute u = 2x² − x, solve u² − 9u + 18 = 0, then back-substitute."]],"qs":[["The general form of a quadratic equation is",["ax + b = 0","ax² + bx + c = 0","x³ + 1 = 0","x² − 1"],1,"Degree 2 with a ≠ 0."],["The roots of x² − 5x + 6 = 0 are",["1, 6","2, 3","−2, −3","3, 5"],1,"(x − 2)(x − 3) = 0."],["The roots of 5x² + 13x + 6 = 0 are",["−2, −3/5","2, 3/5","−2, 3/5","−1, −6/5"],0,"Δ = 169 − 120 = 49. x = (−13 ± 7)/10 = −3/5 or −2."],["The solutions of −4x² − 2x + 12 = 0 are",["3/2, −2","−3/2, 2","3, −2","3/2, 2"],0,"Divide by −2: 2x² + x − 6 = 0, so (2x − 3)(x + 2) = 0."],["Solving 2x² + 8x − 6 = 0 by completing the square gives",["x = −2 ± √7","x = 2 ± √7","x = −4 ± √7","x = −2 ± 7"],0,"x² + 4x = 3, so (x + 2)² = 7."],["The roots of (2x² − x)² − 9(2x² − x) + 18 = 0 are",["−3/2, −1, 3/2, 2","−1, 3/2 only","−3/2, 2 only","1, 2, 3, 6"],0,"u = 3 or 6 with u = 2x² − x. 2x²−x−3 = 0: x = 3/2, −1. 2x²−x−6 = 0: x = 2, −3/2."],["The graph of a quadratic equation is a",["Line","Circle","Parabola","Hyperbola"],2,"Standard result."],["The axis of symmetry of y = ax² + bx + c is",["x = b/2a","x = −b/2a","y = −b/2a","y = b/2a"],1,"Vertex x-coordinate = −b/2a."],["If a = 0 in ax² + bx + c = 0, the equation becomes",["Quadratic","Linear","Cubic","Constant"],1,"Only bx + c remains."],["Completing the square can be used to",["Factorise","Find roots","Graph easily","All of the above"],3,"It does all three."],["The quadratic formula is",["(−b ± √(b²−4ac))/2a","(b ± √(b²−4ac))/2a","(−b ± √(b²+4ac))/2a","(b ± √(b²+4ac))/2a"],0,"Standard result."],["A quadratic equation has how many roots?",["One","Two","Three","Infinite"],1,"Degree 2 gives two roots (counting repeats and complex ones)."],["The roots of x² + 1 = 0 are",["±1","±i","±2","0"],1,"x² = −1, so x = ±i."],["Find the roots of x² + 4x + 13 = 0.",["−2 ± 3i","2 ± 3i","−4 ± 3i","−2 ± 9i"],0,"Δ = 16 − 52 = −36. x = (−4 ± 6i)/2 = −2 ± 3i."]]},{"id":"roots","title":"Sum & Product of Roots, Symmetric Functions","src":["Theory of quadratic equation.pdf","MTH 101_Elementary Mathematics_I (2).pdf","Maths MCQ.pdf (Quadratics)","Maths Exercise.pdf"],"notes":"\n<h3>Sum and product</h3>\n<p>If r₁, r₂ are the roots of ax² + bx + c = 0:</p>\n<div class=\"box\">r₁ + r₂ = −b/a &nbsp;&nbsp; and &nbsp;&nbsp; r₁r₂ = c/a</div>\n<p>Because (x − r₁)(x − r₂) = x² − (r₁ + r₂)x + r₁r₂.</p>\n<p><b>To build an equation from its roots:</b> x² − (sum)x + (product) = 0.</p>\n<h3>Symmetric functions</h3>\n<p>A function is <b>symmetric</b> if swapping r₁ and r₂ leaves it unchanged (or only flips its sign). You can evaluate these without finding the roots.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>r₁² + r₂² = (r₁ + r₂)² − 2r₁r₂</li>\n<li>(r₁ − r₂)² = (r₁ + r₂)² − 4r₁r₂</li>\n<li>r₁³ + r₂³ = (r₁ + r₂)³ − 3r₁r₂(r₁ + r₂)</li>\n<li>1/r₁ + 1/r₂ = (r₁ + r₂)/(r₁r₂)</li>\n<li>r₁² − r₂² = (r₁ + r₂)(r₁ − r₂)</li>\n</ul>\n<p><i>Not symmetric:</i> r₁² + 2r₂².</p>\n<h3>Worked examples</h3>\n<p><b>Ex 1.</b> 3x² − 10x − 4 = 0: sum = 10/3, product = −4/3, so r₁² + r₂² = 100/9 + 8/3 = 124/9.</p>\n<p><b>Ex 2.</b> Roots √3 − 2 and √3 + 2: sum = 2√3, product = −1, equation x² − 2√3x − 1 = 0.</p>\n<p><b>Ex 3.</b> 3x² + 5x − 1 = 0: equation with roots r₁², r₂² is 9x² − 31x + 1 = 0. Equation with roots 1/r₁, 1/r₂ is x² − 5x − 3 = 0.</p>\n<p><b>Proof (one root is 3× the other).</b> For 2x² + bx + c = 0 let roots be α and 3α. Then 4α = −b/2 and 3α² = c/2. Eliminate α to get 3b² = 32c.</p>","cards":[["Sum of the roots of ax² + bx + c = 0?","−b/a"],["Product of the roots?","c/a"],["Equation with a given sum S and product P?","x² − Sx + P = 0"],["r₁² + r₂² in terms of sum and product?","(r₁ + r₂)² − 2r₁r₂"],["(r₁ − r₂)² in terms of sum and product?","(r₁ + r₂)² − 4r₁r₂"],["r₁³ + r₂³ in terms of sum and product?","(r₁ + r₂)³ − 3r₁r₂(r₁ + r₂)"],["1/r₁ + 1/r₂ in terms of sum and product?","(r₁ + r₂)/(r₁r₂)"],["What makes a function of r₁, r₂ symmetric?","Swapping r₁ and r₂ leaves it unchanged (or only multiplies it by −1)."],["Is r₁² + 2r₂² symmetric?","No. Swapping the roots changes it."],["Roots of 3x² − 7x + 2 = 0: product?","c/a = 2/3"],["Roots of 5x² + 10x − 3 = 0: sum?","−b/a = −10/5 = −2"],["Equation with roots 3 and 4?","x² − 7x + 12 = 0"],["If one root is three times the other in 2x²+bx+c=0, what is proved?","3b² = 32c"]],"qs":[["The sum of the roots of ax² + bx + c = 0 is",["c/a","b/a","−b/a","−c/a"],2,"r₁ + r₂ = −b/a."],["The product of the roots of ax² + bx + c = 0 is",["b/a","−b/a","c/a","−c/a"],2,"r₁r₂ = c/a."],["For 3x² − 7x + 2 = 0, the product of the roots is",["2/3","−2/3","7/3","−7/3"],0,"c/a = 2/3."],["For 5x² + 10x − 3 = 0, the sum of the roots is",["2","−2","−3/5","3/5"],1,"−b/a = −10/5 = −2."],["If the sum and product of roots are 5 and 6, the equation is",["x² − 5x + 6 = 0","x² + 5x + 6 = 0","x² − 6x + 5 = 0","x² + 6x − 5 = 0"],0,"x² − (sum)x + product."],["The equation with roots 3 and 4 in standard form is",["x² + 7x + 12 = 0","x² − 7x + 12 = 0","x² − 12x + 7 = 0","x² − x + 12 = 0"],1,"Sum 7, product 12."],["The equation with roots −5 and −1/3 (integer coefficients) is",["3x² + 16x + 5 = 0","3x² − 16x + 5 = 0","x² + 16x + 5 = 0","3x² + 14x − 5 = 0"],0,"Sum = −16/3, product = 5/3. x² + (16/3)x + 5/3 = 0, times 3."],["The equation with roots √2 − 3 and √2 + 3 is",["x² − 2√2x − 7 = 0","x² + 2√2x − 7 = 0","x² − 2√2x + 7 = 0","x² − 2√2x − 1 = 0"],0,"Sum 2√2, product 2 − 9 = −7."],["If α, β are the roots of 3x² + 5x − 2 = 0, then α² + β² =",["25/9","37/9","31/9","13/9"],1,"Sum = −5/3, product = −2/3. 25/9 + 4/3 = 37/9."],["If p, q are the roots of 2x² − x − 4 = 0, then p³ + q³ =",["25/8","−25/8","9/8","7/8"],0,"Sum = ½, product = −2. (½)³ − 3(−2)(½) = 1/8 + 3 = 25/8."],["If α, β are the roots of 2x² + 8x + 7 = 0, then α² + β² =",["9","10","−5","22"],0,"Sum = −4, product = 7/2. 16 − 7 = 9."],["If α, β are the roots of 2x² − 3x − 2 = 0, then (α+β)/β + (β+α)/α =",["−9/4","9/4","−3/2","3/2"],0,"(α+β)(1/α + 1/β) = (α+β)²/(αβ) = (9/4)/(−1) = −9/4."],["If α, β are the roots of 2x² − 3x − 2 = 0, then 1/(α²β) + 1/(β²α) =",["3/2","−3/2","9/4","1"],0,"(α + β)/(αβ)² = (3/2)/1 = 3/2."],["If r₁, r₂ are the roots of 3x² − 10x − 4 = 0, then r₁² + r₂² =",["100/9","124/9","148/9","20/3"],1,"(10/3)² − 2(−4/3) = 100/9 + 24/9 = 124/9."],["If r₁, r₂ are roots of 3x² + 5x − 1 = 0, the equation with roots r₁², r₂² is",["9x² − 31x + 1 = 0","9x² + 31x + 1 = 0","3x² − 31x + 1 = 0","9x² − 25x + 1 = 0"],0,"Sum of squares = 31/9, product = 1/9."],["If r₁, r₂ are roots of 3x² + 5x − 1 = 0, the equation with roots 1/r₁, 1/r₂ is",["x² − 5x − 3 = 0","x² + 5x − 3 = 0","x² − 5x + 3 = 0","3x² − 5x − 1 = 0"],0,"Sum = (−5/3)/(−1/3) = 5, product = 1/(−1/3) = −3."],["Which function is symmetric in r₁ and r₂?",["r₁² + 2r₂²","r₁² + r₂²","r₁ + 2r₂","r₁/2 + r₂"],1,"Swapping gives the same expression."],["Which of these correctly gives r₁² + r₂²?",["(r₁+r₂)² + 2r₁r₂","(r₁+r₂)² − 2r₁r₂","(r₁−r₂)² − 2r₁r₂","(r₁+r₂)² − 4r₁r₂"],1,"Expand (r₁ + r₂)² and subtract 2r₁r₂."]]},{"id":"disc","title":"Discriminant & Nature of Roots","src":["Theory of quadratic equation.pdf","MTH 101_Elementary Mathematics_I (2).pdf","Maths MCQ.pdf (Quadratics)"],"notes":"\n<h3>The discriminant</h3>\n<div class=\"box\">Δ = b² − 4ac</div>\n<table>\n<tr><th>Δ</th><th>Roots</th></tr>\n<tr><td>Δ &gt; 0</td><td>Two distinct real roots</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Δ = 0</td><td>Two equal real roots (a repeated / double root)</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Δ &lt; 0</td><td>Two complex conjugate roots</td></tr>\n</table>\n<p>For Δ &lt; 0: x = [−b ± i√|Δ|] / 2a.</p>\n<h3>Worked examples</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>x² − 5x + 6 = 0: Δ = 25 − 24 = 1 &gt; 0 → roots 3 and 2.</li>\n<li>x² − 4x + 4 = 0: Δ = 0 → double root x = 2.</li>\n<li>x² + 4x + 5 = 0: Δ = −4 &lt; 0 → roots −2 ± i.</li>\n<li>3x² − 2x + 4 = 0: Δ = 4 − 48 = −44 &lt; 0 → complex roots.</li>\n<li>4x² − 28x + 49 = 0: Δ = 784 − 784 = 0 → equal roots (x = 7/2).</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Finding unknown constants</h3>\n<p><b>Equal roots ⇒ Δ = 0.</b></p>\n<p>x² + px + 4 = 0 has equal roots when p² − 16 = 0, so p = ±4.</p>\n<p>x² − 3x + 1 = p(x − 3) rearranges to x² − (3 + p)x + (1 + 3p) = 0. Δ = 0 gives p² − 6p + 5 = 0, so p = 1 or 5.</p>","cards":[["Formula for the discriminant?","Δ = b² − 4ac"],["Δ > 0 means…","Two distinct real roots."],["Δ = 0 means…","Two equal real roots (a double root)."],["Δ < 0 means…","Two complex conjugate roots (no real roots)."],["Roots when Δ < 0?","x = (−b ± i√|Δ|)/2a"],["Condition for equal roots?","Δ = 0"],["Nature of roots of x² − 4x + 4 = 0?","Δ = 0, equal real roots x = 2."],["Nature of roots of 3x² − 2x + 4 = 0?","Δ = −44 < 0, complex roots."],["Nature of roots of 4x² − 28x + 49 = 0?","Δ = 0, equal real roots x = 7/2."],["Values of p for x² + px + 4 = 0 to have equal roots?","p² = 16, so p = ±4."],["What does the nature of roots depend on?","The discriminant."]],"qs":[["The discriminant of ax² + bx + c = 0 is",["b² − 4ac","b² + 4ac","2a","a²"],0,"Δ = b² − 4ac."],["If the discriminant is zero, the roots are",["Imaginary","Unequal","Equal","Zero"],2,"√0 = 0, so both roots are −b/2a."],["If Δ < 0, the roots are",["Real and equal","Real and unequal","Complex","Zero"],2,"The square root of a negative number is imaginary."],["If the roots are real and unequal, then",["Δ < 0","Δ = 0","Δ > 0","Δ = 1"],2,"Positive discriminant."],["If the roots are equal, then",["Δ > 0","Δ = 0","Δ < 0","Δ ≠ 0"],1,"Equal roots mean Δ = 0."],["The nature of the roots of 3x² − 2x + 4 = 0 is",["Real and distinct","Real and equal","Complex","Rational"],2,"Δ = 4 − 48 = −44 < 0."],["The nature of the roots of 4x² − 28x + 49 = 0 is",["Real and distinct","Real and equal","Complex","Irrational"],1,"Δ = 784 − 784 = 0."],["The roots of x² + 4x + 5 = 0 are",["−2 ± i","2 ± i","−4 ± i","−2 ± 2i"],0,"Δ = −4. x = (−4 ± 2i)/2 = −2 ± i."],["The value of Δ for x² − 5x + 6 = 0 is",["1","11","25","49"],0,"25 − 24 = 1."],["x² + px + 4 = 0 has equal roots. The possible p are",["±2","±4","±8","±16"],1,"p² − 16 = 0."],["x² − 3x + 1 = p(x − 3) has equal roots. The values of p are",["1 and 5","−1 and −5","1 and −5","2 and 3"],0,"Δ = (3+p)² − 4(1+3p) = p² − 6p + 5 = 0."],["Which equation has two distinct real roots?",["x² − 4x + 4 = 0","x² + 2x + 5 = 0","x² − 5x + 6 = 0","x² + 1 = 0"],2,"Δ values: 0, −16, 1, −4. Only the third is positive."]]},{"id":"binomial","title":"Binomial Theorem","src":["Maths Exercise.pdf"],"notes":"\n<h3>Positive whole-number powers</h3>\n<div class=\"box\">(a + b)<sup>n</sup> = Σ C(n, k) a<sup>n−k</sup> b<sup>k</sup>, k = 0 … n<br>C(n, k) = n! / [k!(n − k)!]</div>\n<ul>\n<li>The (k + 1)th term is C(n, k) a<sup>n−k</sup> b<sup>k</sup>.</li>\n<li>The expansion has n + 1 terms.</li>\n<li>For (1 + x)<sup>n</sup>: 1 + nx + C(n,2)x² + C(n,3)x³ + …</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Negative or fractional powers</h3>\n<div class=\"box\">(1 + x)<sup>n</sup> = 1 + nx + n(n − 1)/2! x² + n(n − 1)(n − 2)/3! x³ + …, valid for |x| &lt; 1</div>\n<p>The series never stops, because n is not a positive integer.</p>\n<h3>Worked examples</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>(1 + x)<sup>15</sup> ascending: 1 + 15x + 105x² + …</li>\n<li>(2 + x)<sup>5</sup> ascending: 32 + 80x + 80x² + …</li>\n<li>(2 + x)<sup>4</sup> = 16 + 32x + 24x² + 8x³ + x⁴</li>\n<li>x⁵ term of (1 + x)<sup>10</sup>: C(10,5) = 252, so 252x⁵.</li>\n<li>Coefficient of x² in (3 − x)<sup>10</sup>: C(10,2)·3⁸ = 45 × 6561 = 295 245.</li>\n<li>Coefficient of z⁴ in (5 + z)<sup>8</sup>: C(8,4)·5⁴ = 70 × 625 = 43 750.</li>\n<li>(1 − x)<sup>−½</sup> = 1 + ½x + 3/8 x² + 5/16 x³ + …</li>\n<li>(1 − x)<sup>−2</sup>(1 + x)<sup>−2</sup> = (1 − x²)<sup>−2</sup> = 1 + 2x² + 3x⁴ + …, so the coefficient of x³ is 0.</li>\n</ul>","cards":[["Binomial theorem for positive integer n?","(a+b)ⁿ = Σ C(n,k) aⁿ⁻ᵏ bᵏ, k = 0 to n."],["Formula for C(n, k)?","n! / [k!(n − k)!]"],["Number of terms in (a + b)ⁿ?","n + 1"],["General (k+1)th term of (a + b)ⁿ?","C(n,k) aⁿ⁻ᵏ bᵏ"],["Series for (1 + x)ⁿ when n is not a positive integer?","1 + nx + n(n−1)/2! x² + n(n−1)(n−2)/3! x³ + …, valid for |x| < 1."],["First three terms of (1 + x)¹⁵?","1 + 15x + 105x²"],["First three terms of (2 + x)⁵ in ascending powers?","32 + 80x + 80x²"],["Expand (2 + x)⁴.","16 + 32x + 24x² + 8x³ + x⁴"],["x⁵ term of (1 + x)¹⁰?","252x⁵"],["First four terms of (1 − x)^(−1/2)?","1 + x/2 + 3x²/8 + 5x³/16"],["Coefficient of x² in (3 − x)¹⁰?","C(10,2)·3⁸ = 45 × 6561 = 295 245"],["Coefficient of z⁴ in (5 + z)⁸?","C(8,4)·5⁴ = 43 750"],["Why does (1+x)⁻ⁿ expand forever?","Because n is not a positive integer, so no coefficient ever becomes 0."]],"qs":[["The number of terms in the expansion of (a + b)¹⁰ is",["10","11","12","20"],1,"n + 1 = 11."],["The first three terms of (1 + x)¹⁵ in ascending order are",["1 + 15x + 105x²","1 + 15x + 15x²","1 + 15x + 210x²","15 + 105x + x²"],0,"C(15,0) = 1, C(15,1) = 15, C(15,2) = 105."],["The first three terms of (2 + x)⁵ in ascending powers of x are",["32 + 80x + 80x²","32 + 40x + 10x²","16 + 40x + 40x²","32 + 80x + 40x²"],0,"2⁵ = 32; 5·2⁴ = 80; 10·2³ = 80."],["The x⁵ term in the expansion of (1 + x)¹⁰ is",["210x⁵","252x⁵","120x⁵","45x⁵"],1,"C(10,5) = 252."],["The coefficient of x² in (3 − x)¹⁰ is",["405","3645","295 245","59 049"],2,"C(10,2)·3⁸·(−1)² = 45 × 6561 = 295 245."],["The coefficient of z⁴ in (5 + z)⁸ is",["43 750","8750","350","56 250"],0,"C(8,4)·5⁴ = 70 × 625."],["(2 + x)⁴ =",["16 + 32x + 24x² + 8x³ + x⁴","16 + 8x + 6x² + 4x³ + x⁴","8 + 24x + 32x² + 16x³ + x⁴","16 + 32x + 32x² + 8x³ + x⁴"],0,"Coefficients C(4,k)·2⁴⁻ᵏ: 16, 32, 24, 8, 1."],["The first four terms of (1 − x)^(−1/2) are",["1 + x/2 + 3x²/8 + 5x³/16","1 − x/2 + 3x²/8 − 5x³/16","1 + x/2 + x²/4 + x³/8","1 + x + x² + x³"],0,"1 + (−½)(−x) + [(−½)(−3/2)/2](x²) + [(−½)(−3/2)(−5/2)/6](−x)³."],["The coefficient of x³ in (1 − x)⁻²(1 + x)⁻² is",["0","2","3","−2"],0,"The product is (1 − x²)⁻² = 1 + 2x² + 3x⁴ + …, which has only even powers."],["C(8, 3) =",["24","56","40","336"],1,"8·7·6/(3·2·1) = 56."],["The expansion of (1 + x)⁻³ is",["Finite","Infinite for |x| < 1","Has 4 terms","Always zero"],1,"Negative power, so the series never terminates."],["The (k + 1)th term of (a + b)ⁿ is",["C(n,k)aᵏbⁿ⁻ᵏ","C(n,k)aⁿ⁻ᵏbᵏ","C(n,k+1)aⁿ⁻ᵏbᵏ","nᵏaᵏ"],1,"Standard general-term formula."]]},{"id":"complex","title":"Complex Numbers","src":["Maths MCQ.pdf (Complex Numbers)","Maths Exercise.pdf"],"notes":"\n<h3>Basics</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><b>i = √−1</b>, so i² = −1, i³ = −i, i⁴ = 1. Powers of i cycle with period 4.</li>\n<li><b>Standard form</b>: z = a + bi, with real part a and imaginary part b (b, not bi).</li>\n<li><b>Conjugate</b> of a + bi is a − bi.</li>\n<li><b>Modulus</b>: |a + bi| = √(a² + b²). It is always ≥ 0.</li>\n<li>z·z̄ = |z|² (real and non-negative).</li>\n<li><b>Argument</b>: the angle of z with the positive real axis.</li>\n<li>|z| = 1 means z lies on the unit circle.</li>\n<li>Purely imaginary: a = 0 (e.g. 5i). Purely real: b = 0.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Operations</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>Add: (a + bi) + (c + di) = (a + c) + (b + d)i</li>\n<li>Multiply: (a + bi)(c + di) = (ac − bd) + (ad + bc)i</li>\n<li>Divide: multiply top and bottom by the conjugate of the denominator.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Worked examples</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>(1 + i)² = 1 + 2i − 1 = 2i</li>\n<li>(4 − 2i)² = 12 − 16i and (3 + 2i)³ = −9 + 46i, so the sum is <b>3 + 30i</b>.</li>\n<li>(2 + 3i)⁵ = 122 − 597i</li>\n<li>(−3 + 2i)/(3 − 6i) = (−3 + 2i)(3 + 6i)/45 = (−21 − 12i)/45 = −7/15 − 4i/15</li>\n<li>[(1 + i)/(1 − i)]³: (1+i)/(1−i) = i, so the answer is i³ = <b>−i</b></li>\n<li>|(2 − 3i)(−1 + 7i)| = √13 · √50 = √650 = 5√26</li>\n</ul>","cards":[["What is i²?","−1"],["Powers of i: i³ and i⁴?","i³ = −i, i⁴ = 1 (cycle repeats every 4)."],["Standard form of a complex number?","a + bi"],["Conjugate of a + bi?","a − bi"],["Modulus of a + bi?","√(a² + b²)"],["Value of |3 + 4i|?","5"],["z·z̄ equals…","|z|²"],["Product (a+bi)(c+di)?","(ac − bd) + (ad + bc)i"],["How do you divide complex numbers?","Multiply numerator and denominator by the conjugate of the denominator."],["Imaginary part of 2 − 5i?","−5"],["What does |z| = 1 describe?","The unit circle."],["What is the argument of a complex number?","The angle between the number (as a vector) and the positive real axis."],["i¹⁰ = ?","i¹⁰ = (i⁴)²·i² = −1"],["(1 + i)² = ?","2i"],["(1 + i)/(1 − i) = ?","i"]],"qs":[["i² =",["1","−1","i","0"],1,"By definition."],["i⁴ =",["−1","1","i","0"],1,"i² · i² = (−1)(−1) = 1."],["i¹⁰ =",["−1","1","i","−i"],0,"i¹⁰ = (i⁴)²·i² = −1."],["The standard form of a complex number is",["a + i","a + ib","ib","ai"],1,"z = a + bi."],["The conjugate of 3 + 4i is",["3 − 4i","−3 + 4i","4 + 3i","−3 − 4i"],0,"Flip the sign of the imaginary part."],["|3 + 4i| =",["5","7","25","1"],0,"√(9 + 16) = 5."],["The real part of 2 − 5i is",["−5","5","2","i"],2,"Real part is the number without i."],["The imaginary part of 2 − 5i is",["2","−5","5","i"],1,"It is the coefficient of i."],["(1 + i)² =",["2i","1 + i","1 − i","2"],0,"1 + 2i + i² = 2i."],["Which is purely imaginary?",["3 + 2i","4","5i","2 − 3i"],2,"Real part is 0."],["Which is purely real?",["3i","2 + i","5","1 − i"],2,"Imaginary part is 0."],["z · z̄ =",["z","z̄","|z|²","z²"],2,"(a + bi)(a − bi) = a² + b²."],["The modulus of a complex number is always",["Negative","Zero","Non-negative","Imaginary"],2,"It is a distance, so ≥ 0."],["If |z| = 1 then z lies on the",["Real axis","Imaginary axis","Unit circle","Origin only"],2,"Distance 1 from the origin."],["(a + bi)(c + di) =",["ac + bd","(ac − bd) + (ad + bc)i","(a + c) + (b + d)i","i(ab + cd)"],1,"Expand and use i² = −1."],["If z = 0, then",["z is imaginary only","z is real only","Both real and imaginary parts are 0","z is undefined"],2,"0 + 0i."],["Evaluate (4 − 2i)² + (3 + 2i)³.",["3 + 30i","7 + 18i","3 − 30i","−5 + 46i"],0,"(4−2i)² = 16 − 16i − 4 = 12 − 16i. (3+2i)³ = −9 + 46i. Sum: 3 + 30i."],["Express (2 + 3i)⁵ in the form a + ib.",["122 − 597i","−122 + 597i","122 + 597i","−597 + 122i"],0,"(2+3i)² = −5 + 12i; (−5+12i)² = −119 − 120i; times (2+3i) = 122 − 597i."],["Evaluate (−3 + 2i)/(3 − 6i).",["−7/15 − 4i/15","7/15 + 4i/15","−1/3 + i/3","−7/45 − 4i/45"],0,"Multiply by (3+6i)/(3+6i): numerator −21 − 12i, denominator 45."],["Express [(1 + i)/(1 − i)]³ in the form A + iB.",["−i","i","1","−1"],0,"(1+i)/(1−i) = i, and i³ = −i."],["The modulus of (2 − 3i)(−1 + 7i) is",["√650","25","13","50"],0,"√13 · √50 = √650 = 5√26 ≈ 25.5."],["Complex numbers are closed under",["Addition only","Multiplication only","Addition and multiplication","Division by zero"],2,"Sums and products of complex numbers are complex."]]},{"id":"trig","title":"Trigonometry: Ratios, Identities & Functions","src":["Maths MCQ.pdf (Trigonometry; Trig Ratios & Functions)"],"notes":"\n<h3>Core definitions</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>tan θ = sin θ / cos θ &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; cot θ = cos θ / sin θ</li>\n<li>sec θ = 1 / cos θ &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; csc θ = 1 / sin θ</li>\n<li><b>sin²θ + cos²θ = 1</b></li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Special angle values</h3>\n<table>\n<tr><th>θ</th><th>0°</th><th>30°</th><th>45°</th><th>60°</th><th>90°</th></tr>\n<tr><td>sin</td><td>0</td><td>1/2</td><td>√2/2</td><td>√3/2</td><td>1</td></tr>\n<tr><td>cos</td><td>1</td><td>√3/2</td><td>√2/2</td><td>1/2</td><td>0</td></tr>\n<tr><td>tan</td><td>0</td><td>1/√3</td><td>1</td><td>√3</td><td>undefined</td></tr>\n</table>\n<p>Also: cos 180° = −1, sin 180° = 0, tan 180° = 0, sin 270° = −1, cos 270° = 0.</p>\n<h3>Symmetry and complementary angles</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>sin(−θ) = −sin θ (odd), tan(−θ) = −tan θ (odd), cos(−θ) = cos θ (even).</li>\n<li>sin(90° − θ) = cos θ, cos(90° − θ) = sin θ, tan(90° − θ) = cot θ.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Double angles</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>sin 2x = 2 sin x cos x</li>\n<li>cos 2x = cos²x − sin²x</li>\n<li>tan 2x = 2 tan x / (1 − tan²x)</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Graph facts</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>sin x and cos x: period 2π, domain ℝ, range [−1, 1].</li>\n<li>tan x: period π, range ℝ, undefined when cos x = 0.</li>\n<li>Max of sin x = 1; min of cos x = −1.</li>\n</ul>","cards":[["Fundamental Pythagorean identity?","sin²θ + cos²θ = 1"],["tan θ in terms of sin and cos?","sin θ / cos θ"],["cot θ = ?","cos θ / sin θ"],["sec θ = ?","1 / cos θ"],["csc θ = ?","1 / sin θ"],["sin 30°, cos 60°?","Both 1/2"],["sin 45° and cos 45°?","Both √2/2 (so sin²45° = 1/2)"],["tan 45°? tan 60°?","1; √3"],["sin 90°, cos 0°?","1, 1"],["cos 180°? sin 270°?","−1; −1"],["Is sin x odd or even?","Odd: sin(−x) = −sin x."],["Is cos x odd or even?","Even: cos(−x) = cos x."],["sin(90° − θ) = ?","cos θ"],["cos(90° − θ) = ?","sin θ"],["tan(90° − θ) = ?","cot θ"],["Period of sin x and cos x? Of tan x?","2π; π"],["Range of sin x / cos x? Range of tan x?","[−1, 1]; all real numbers."],["sin 2x = ?","2 sin x cos x"],["cos 2x = ?","cos²x − sin²x"],["tan 2x = ?","2 tan x / (1 − tan²x)"],["When is tan x undefined?","When cos x = 0 (e.g. x = 90°)."],["csc 30°? sec 60°?","2; 2"],["cot 45°?","1"]],"qs":[["sin²θ + cos²θ =",["0","1","2","tan θ"],1,"Fundamental identity."],["tan θ =",["sin θ / cos θ","cos θ / sin θ","1 / sin θ","1 / cos θ"],0,"By definition."],["sin 30° =",["1","√3/2","1/2","0"],2,"Special angle."],["cos 0° =",["0","1","−1","1/2"],1,"Special angle."],["tan 45° =",["0","1","√3","∞"],1,"Opposite = adjacent."],["sin 90° =",["0","1","−1","Undefined"],1,"Special angle."],["cos 60° =",["1","1/2","√3/2","0"],1,"Special angle."],["tan 0° =",["0","1","∞","Undefined"],0,"sin 0 / cos 0 = 0/1."],["sin(−θ) =",["sin θ","−sin θ","cos θ","−cos θ"],1,"Sine is odd."],["cos(−θ) =",["−cos θ","sin θ","cos θ","−sin θ"],2,"Cosine is even."],["tan(−θ) =",["tan θ","−tan θ","cot θ","−cot θ"],1,"Tangent is odd."],["sin(90° − θ) =",["sin θ","cos θ","tan θ","sec θ"],1,"Complementary-angle identity."],["cos(90° − θ) =",["sin θ","cos θ","tan θ","cot θ"],0,"Complementary-angle identity."],["tan(90° − θ) =",["tan θ","cot θ","sec θ","csc θ"],1,"Complementary-angle identity."],["The period of sin x is",["π","2π","π/2","4π"],1,"Repeats every 2π."],["The period of tan x is",["2π","π","π/2","4π"],1,"Repeats every π."],["sin²45° =",["1","1/2","1/4","√2"],1,"(√2/2)² = 1/2."],["cos²60° =",["1","1/2","1/4","√3/2"],2,"(1/2)² = 1/4."],["tan 60° =",["1","√3","1/√3","0"],1,"Special angle."],["sin 0° =",["1","0","−1","Undefined"],1,"Special angle."],["cos 180° =",["1","−1","0","Undefined"],1,"Point (−1, 0) on the unit circle."],["tan 180° =",["0","1","∞","Undefined"],0,"sin 180° / cos 180° = 0/(−1) = 0."],["sin 270° =",["1","−1","0","Undefined"],1,"Bottom of the unit circle."],["cos 270° =",["1","−1","0","Undefined"],2,"Point (0, −1)."],["tan 90° is",["0","1","−1","Undefined"],3,"cos 90° = 0, so division by zero."],["The reciprocal of sin θ is",["sec θ","cos θ","csc θ","tan θ"],2,"csc θ = 1/sin θ."],["The reciprocal of cos θ is",["tan θ","sec θ","csc θ","cot θ"],1,"sec θ = 1/cos θ."],["csc 30° =",["1","2","√3","1/2"],1,"1/sin 30° = 2."],["sec 60° =",["2","1","1/2","√3"],0,"1/cos 60° = 2."],["cot 45° =",["0","1","√3","1/√3"],1,"1/tan 45°."],["The domain of sin x is",["ℕ","ℤ","ℝ","[0, 2π]"],2,"Defined for every real x."],["The range of cos x is",["[0, 1]","[−1, 1]","(−∞, ∞)","[0, ∞)"],1,"Between −1 and 1."],["The range of tan x is",["[0, 1]","[−1, 1]","(−∞, ∞)","[0, ∞)"],2,"Takes every real value."],["sin x is an",["Even function","Odd function","Periodic constant","Undefined function"],1,"sin(−x) = −sin x."],["cos x is an",["Odd function","Even function","Increasing function","Decreasing function"],1,"cos(−x) = cos x."],["tan x is undefined when",["sin x = 0","cos x = 0","tan x = 1","cot x = 0"],1,"Division by cos x."],["sin 2x equals",["2 sin x cos x","sin²x","2 cos x","tan x"],0,"Double-angle formula."],["cos 2x =",["cos²x − sin²x","2 cos x","sin²x","1 − sin x"],0,"Double-angle formula."],["tan 2x =",["2 tan x","tan²x","2 tan x / (1 − tan²x)","(1 − tan²x) / 2 tan x"],2,"Double-angle formula."],["The graph of sin x is a",["Parabola","Straight line","Periodic wave","Hyperbola"],2,"Smooth repeating wave."],["The maximum value of sin x is",["0","1","−1","∞"],1,"Highest point on the wave."],["The minimum value of cos x is",["−1","0","1","∞"],0,"Lowest point on the wave."],["cot x =",["sin x / cos x","cos x / sin x","1 / cos x","1 / sin x"],1,"Reciprocal of tan x."]]},{"id":"induction","title":"Mathematical Induction","src":["Maths MCQ.pdf (Mathematical Induction)"],"notes":"\n<h3>The idea</h3>\n<p>Induction proves a statement P(n) for <b>all natural numbers n</b>. It rests on the fact that the natural numbers are <b>well ordered</b>: like falling dominoes, if the first falls and each one knocks over the next, they all fall.</p>\n<h3>Steps</h3>\n<ol>\n<li><b>Base case</b>: show P(n) is true for the starting value (usually n = 1, or 0).</li>\n<li><b>Induction hypothesis</b>: <i>assume</i> P(k) is true for some k.</li>\n<li><b>Induction step</b>: using P(k), prove P(k + 1).</li>\n<li><b>Conclusion</b>: by induction, P(n) holds for all natural numbers n.</li>\n</ol>\n<ul>\n<li>If the base case is false, the proof fails. If the induction step fails, the proof is incomplete.</li>\n<li><b>Strong induction</b> assumes P(j) for <i>all</i> j ≤ k, which is useful when the next case needs several previous cases.</li>\n<li>Induction is especially useful for formulas involving sums, and is common in number theory and sequences.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Example: 1 + 2 + … + n = n(n + 1)/2</h3>\n<p><b>Base:</b> n = 1: 1 = 1·2/2 ✓.</p>\n<p><b>Hypothesis:</b> assume 1 + … + k = k(k + 1)/2.</p>\n<p><b>Step:</b> 1 + … + k + (k + 1) = k(k + 1)/2 + (k + 1) = (k + 1)(k + 2)/2, which is the formula with n = k + 1 ✓.</p>\n<p><b>Conclusion:</b> true for all n ∈ ℕ.</p>","cards":[["What does induction prove statements about?","All natural numbers n."],["First step of an induction proof?","Verify the base case (usually n = 1 or 0)."],["What is the induction hypothesis?","An assumption that P(k) is true for some k."],["What does the induction step prove?","P(k + 1), using the assumption P(k)."],["What principle underlies induction?","The natural numbers are well ordered."],["What if the base case is false?","The proof is invalid."],["What if the induction step fails?","The proof is incomplete."],["What does strong induction assume?","P(j) is true for all j ≤ k."],["When is strong induction useful?","When proving P(k+1) needs several earlier cases, not just P(k)."],["Name the four parts of an induction proof.","Base case, hypothesis, induction step, conclusion."],["Induction is especially useful for formulas involving…","Sums (and sequences, number theory)."],["Induction works on sequences indexed by…","The natural numbers."],["What does the conclusion step do?","Completes the proof: P(n) holds for all natural numbers n."]],"qs":[["Mathematical induction is mainly used to prove results for",["Real numbers","Natural numbers","Complex numbers","Matrices"],1,"It works over the well-ordered naturals."],["The first step in mathematical induction is",["Hypothesis","Conclusion","Base case","Induction step"],2,"Verify the starting value first."],["In the induction hypothesis, we assume the statement is true for",["n only","n + 1","k, for some k","All n"],2,"We assume P(k) for an arbitrary k (the book's n). Assuming it for all n would be circular."],["If the base case is false, the proof is",["Valid","Invalid","Partial","Correct"],1,"Without a true starting point nothing follows."],["The induction step proves P(k + 1) using",["P(1)","P(k)","P(k − 1)","P(0)"],1,"Assume P(k), derive P(k + 1)."],["Mathematical induction is based on the principle of",["Contradiction","Well ordering","Continuity","Integration"],1,"The naturals are well ordered."],["Strong induction assumes truth for",["n only","n + 1 only","All k ≤ n","All k ≥ n"],2,"It uses every earlier case."],["Which is NOT part of an induction proof?",["Base case","Hypothesis","Verification of every n individually","Induction step"],2,"The whole point is to avoid checking every n."],["Failure of the induction step makes the proof",["True","False","Incomplete","Stronger"],2,"It does not disprove the statement, but it does not prove it either."],["Induction applies to sequences indexed by",["Rationals","Reals","Naturals","Complex numbers"],2,"Natural-number indices."],["The induction hypothesis is an",["Axiom","Assumption","Theorem","Definition"],1,"A temporary assumption used in the step."],["The base case is usually verified for",["n = 0 or 1","n = 10","n = 100","Any value"],0,"The smallest value of n in the claim."],["Induction ensures the statement is true for",["One value","Finitely many values","All natural numbers","All real numbers"],2,"Base + step give every n ≥ the base."],["Strong induction is useful when",["Steps are independent","Previous cases are required","The base case fails","Numbers are finite"],1,"The step needs more than just P(k)."],["Induction is especially useful for proving formulas involving",["Integrals","Limits","Sums","Matrices"],2,"Sums like 1 + 2 + … + n."],["The conclusion step completes the",["Base case","Hypothesis","Proof","Assumption"],2,"It states the result for all n."],["In 1 + 2 + … + n = n(n+1)/2, the base case n = 1 gives",["1 = 1","1 = 2","1 = 3","0 = 1"],0,"RHS = 1·2/2 = 1."],["In that proof, adding (k + 1) to k(k+1)/2 gives",["(k+1)(k+2)/2","k(k+2)/2","(k+1)²/2","k(k+1)"],0,"k(k+1)/2 + (k+1) = (k+1)(k/2 + 1) = (k+1)(k+2)/2."]]}];
  const noteStore = window.__NOTES__ || (window.__NOTES__ = {});
  const current = noteStore.MTH101 || { title: 'MTH 101 — Elementary Mathematics I', topics: [] };
  current.title = 'MTH 101 — Elementary Mathematics I';
  current.topics = current.topics || [];
  imported.forEach(topic => {
    const match = current.topics.find(t => t.title === topic.title);
    const htmlSection = { type: 'html', html: topic.notes || '' };
    if (match) { match.sections = match.sections || []; match.sections.push(htmlSection); }
    else current.topics.push({ title: topic.title, sections: [htmlSection] });
  });
  noteStore.MTH101 = current;

  const cardStore = window.__CARDS__ || (window.__CARDS__ = {});
  const deck = cardStore.MTH101 || (cardStore.MTH101 = []);
  const cardKeys = new Set(deck.map(c => String(c.front || c.q || '') + '::' + String(c.u || 'General')));
  imported.forEach(topic => (topic.cards || []).forEach(card => {
    const front = card[0], back = card[1], key = String(front) + '::' + topic.title;
    if (!cardKeys.has(key)) { deck.push({ front, back, u: topic.title }); cardKeys.add(key); }
  }));

  const bank = window.__BANK__ || (window.__BANK__ = []);
  const existingQuestionKeys = new Set(bank.filter(q => q.c === 'MTH101').map(q => String(q.q || '').trim().toLowerCase()));
  const importedIds = new Set(bank.map(q => q.i));
  imported.forEach(topic => (topic.qs || []).forEach((item, index) => {
    const question = String(item[0] || '').trim(), key = question.toLowerCase();
    const id = 'MTH101-UPLOAD-20261026-' + topic.id.toUpperCase() + '-' + String(index + 1).padStart(3, '0');
    if (!question || importedIds.has(id) || existingQuestionKeys.has(key)) return;
    const options = item[1].slice(), answerIndex = Number(item[2]), explanation = String(item[3] || '').trim();
    if (!options.length || !Number.isInteger(answerIndex) || answerIndex < 0 || answerIndex >= options.length || !explanation) return;
    bank.push({ i: id, c: 'MTH101', u: topic.title, s: 'mcq', q: question, o: options, a: answerIndex, e: explanation });
    importedIds.add(id); existingQuestionKeys.add(key);
  }));
})();
